  "storage": "Keep in an airtight jar or the sealed sprinkler tub at room temperature, away from steam and sunlight. Black salt draws moisture, so always use a dry spoon; in the humid monsoon it can cake. No need to refrigerate.",
  "looksLike": "A fine, grey-brown to khaki powder with a sharp, sour, salty smell and a faint sulphur note from black salt. Sold in small cartons or sprinkler packs.",
  "howToPick": "Buy a sealed pack with a recent manufacturing date and FSSAI licence number; the powder should be free-flowing (not caked) and smell sharply sour and spicy. Faded colour or a dull smell means it is old. If you need it gluten-free, check the ingredient list for hing (asafoetida), which in India is usually compounded with wheat flour (FitPlate dish file, dish 59).",
  "shelfLife": "Usually 9–12 months from manufacture unopened (check the pack); best used within 3–4 months of opening, as the aroma fades.",
